/**
 * @page vfs_architecture VFS Architecture
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_object_model Object model
 *
 * The VFS object model separates file-system state from the path state of an
 * execution context. The target class hierarchy is:
 *
 * @startuml {vfs_arch.png} "VFS Core"
 * abstract class vfs_fs_c {
 *   + stat(absolute_path)
 *   + open_directory(absolute_path)
 *   + open_file(absolute_path)
 *   + unlink(absolute_path)
 *   + rename(absolute_oldpath, absolute_newpath)
 *   + mkdir(absolute_path)
 *   + rmdir(absolute_path)
 * }
 * class vfs_overlay_driver_c {
 *   - overlaid_fs: vfs_fs_c *
 *   - mounted_fs[]: vfs_fs_c *
 * }
 * class vfs_root_c {
 *   - current_directory
 *   - path_prefix: const char *
 *   + change_current_directory(path)
 *   + get_current_directory()
 *   + open(path)
 * }
 * abstract class vfs_node_c {
 *   - fs: vfs_fs_c *
 * }
 * abstract class vfs_directory_node_c
 * abstract class vfs_file_node_c
 *
 * vfs_fs_c <|-- vfs_overlay_driver_c
 * vfs_overlay_driver_c <|-- vfs_root_c
 * vfs_node_c <|-- vfs_directory_node_c
 * vfs_node_c <|-- vfs_file_node_c
 * vfs_node_c "*" --> "1" vfs_fs_c : owner
 * vfs_overlay_driver_c "1" o-- "*" vfs_fs_c : mounts
 * @enduml
 *
 * File-system implementations, including non-root overlays, derive from
 * @p vfs_fs_c. A @p vfs_root_c is an overlay specialized as the entry point
 * of an application or sandbox.
 *
 * The process-aware root and global path API are enabled by
 * @p VFS_CFG_ENABLE_DRV_ROOT. Root support requires the overlay driver, but
 * the core VFS API, concrete file systems and non-root overlays can be used
 * with root support disabled.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_concurrency Synchronization status
 *
 * Shared buffer reservations protect temporary paths. Enabling
 * @p VFS_CFG_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION adds one local metadata mutex per overlay
 * object, inherited by root, for mount lookup/updates and CWD copying/commit.
 * It is released before delegation, callbacks, node disposal, or blocking
 * allocation.
 * Rename snapshots both routes together at each layer; root also resolves
 * both inputs against one CWD. No global VFS operation mutex is provided.
 *
 * The option defaults to @p FALSE: mutex fields disappear and local lock
 * helpers become empty macros. Callers then serialize shared state.
 * Kernel pool/reference protection and native backend configuration remain
 * independent. Descriptor ownership is protected locally as described below.
 * Caller integration and simulator concurrency validation cover root/overlay,
 * ROMFS, FatFS/LittleFS wrappers and descriptor ownership under the contracts
 * below. Shared backend synchronization, node ordering and native lifecycle
 * requirements remain the caller's responsibility.
 *
 * Each root allocates one CWD buffer on its first successful chdir using
 * @p chCoreAlloc(). This bounded, nonblocking allocation runs under the
 * metadata mutex to prevent duplicate allocations. Later changes reuse the
 * same buffer. Core storage is permanent and is not reclaimed on disposal;
 * no operation pair is retained for CWD storage. Successful chdir calls take
 * effect in commit order after unlocked validation and node release.
 *
 * Overlay enumeration is a live view. Mount changes may skip or repeat
 * entries in the mount phase; they cannot restart the backing phase or
 * reopen an ended iterator. A new first call restarts both phases.
 *
 * @subsection vfs_arch_handle_ownership Handle ownership and concurrency
 *
 * Each independent node user holds a reference throughout its operation,
 * including waits and access through borrowed stream interfaces. Reference
 * counting protects lifetime; it does not serialize operations. Another
 * owner may release its reference while the operation retains its own.
 * A copied pointer or descriptor lookup alone does not retain the node.
 * Direct methods and convenience APIs follow these same rules. The latter
 * check pointer arguments without reading shared reference counters. Only
 * the reference implementation or a protected descriptor pin changes or
 * inspects those counters. Concurrent reference ownership requires OOP
 * synchronization; @c OOP_USE_NOTHING does not provide it.
 *
 * Callers serialize operations on the same open node unless its driver
 * explicitly permits concurrent use. This includes stat, read/write,
 * seek/position, control, and directory first/next. Duplicated descriptors
 * share a node and its position. Multi-call sequences need caller ordering.
 * Distinct nodes may be used concurrently only when the leaf and its backend
 * support that usage; separate stream nodes can share one backend cursor.
 * Native restrictions on opening or modifying the same file still apply.
 *
 * File systems, prefixes, backing relationships, immutable descriptions, and
 * backend interfaces remain valid through active operations and final node
 * disposal. Native mount/unmount/format and backend reconfiguration require an
 * exclusive lifecycle phase with no affected active operations or live nodes.
 * Unregistering a route does not wait for its active operations to finish.
 *
 * The local synchronization design releases upper VFS metadata protection
 * before driver or node methods, callbacks, reference release/disposal, and
 * blocking allocation. Each leaf supplies its own synchronization. These
 * requirements also apply to custom drivers and callbacks; enabling the option
 * cannot supply synchronization for arbitrary shared backend state.
 *
 * @subsection vfs_arch_leaf_locking Leaf wrapper synchronization
 *
 * With @p VFS_CFG_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION enabled, FatFS uses one module mutex
 * for all volumes, node operations and mount/unmount. LittleFS uses one mutex
 * per driver instance for its native state, mounted flag, node operations and
 * mount/unmount/format. FatFS native reentrancy and LittleFS @c LFS_THREADSAFE
 * are optional. Compound rewind/read and open/truncate acquire the wrapper
 * lock once; recursive kernel mutexes are not required.
 *
 * A leaf mutex spans native calls, storage callbacks and I/O waits. Releasing
 * it while non-reentrant native state is active would permit corruption.
 * The order is leaf wrapper, then optional native/backend protection.
 * Storage callbacks must not reenter VFS. Direct native calls must not overlap
 * wrapper operations. These mutexes do not remove lifecycle or native
 * file-sharing restrictions, nor provide atomic multi-call transactions.
 *
 * Independent LittleFS instances can progress concurrently on independent
 * storage. Shared hardware still needs a shared device synchronization domain.
 * The standard LittleFS HAL binding can supply this through optional native
 * lock hooks and enabled HAL exclusive access. Without those hooks, use an
 * exclusively owned device or synchronized callbacks. Distinct native states
 * must never mount the same storage concurrently.
 *
 * ROMFS immutable data and per-open sessions need no module mutex. Shared
 * custom callback state and aliased stream interfaces retain their backend
 * synchronization contract. CHFS remains unfinished and is not covered by
 * the concurrency guarantee.
 *
 * @subsection vfs_arch_descriptors Descriptor ownership
 *
 * Newlib descriptors each own one node reference. Insertion, lookup/pinning
 * and detachment use short system critical sections. Read/write and fstat
 * retain the selected node before unlocking; I/O and final release happen
 * outside table protection. Close detaches the slot before releasing it, so
 * another open may reuse the number while an earlier operation or disposal
 * still runs. That operation finishes on its original node.
 *
 * The newlib pin is a private, non-dispatching I-class helper for the standard
 * OOP reference counter. Newlib open rejects nodes overriding addref or release
 * with @c ENOTSUP; arbitrary disposal overrides remain supported. OOP reference
 * synchronization must be enabled. This reference/table protection is active
 * independently of @p VFS_CFG_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION, like pool protection.
 * It does not serialize same-handle operations or libc stream state.
 *
 * A sandbox has one host thread owning its active descriptor table. Host
 * registration is allowed only while STOPPED, under exclusive lifecycle
 * ownership. Table references retain nodes throughout syscalls, including
 * getdents scratch waits. Close and cleanup detach before release; dup2 retains
 * the source and replaces the destination before releasing the old node.
 * Host-side mutation of a running sandbox's table remains unsupported.
 *
 * @subsection vfs_arch_callers Caller integration
 *
 * Publish the application-provided @p vfs_root before starting users and
 * keep it stable until they are quiescent. Top-level path calls use that
 * root's shared logical CWD. Shell commands own their opened nodes and local
 * work buffers, but concurrent commands share CWD; a chdir followed by another
 * command is not an atomic sequence. Output stream synchronization belongs
 * to the shared terminal backend.
 *
 * ELF functions accepting a node borrow the caller's reference and require
 * exclusive use of its file position for the entire load or size query.
 * @p sbElfLoadFile() and sandbox dynamic startup instead own a private open
 * node through all reads and waits. The destination region is exclusively
 * owned until loading finishes. Relocation uses the pair's combined capacity,
 * rounded down to whole relocation records, and releases it on every exit.
 *
 * HTTP bindings are initialized before serving requests and keep a stable
 * filesystem pointer until all files are closed. Each HTTP file owns a node
 * reference and is serialized by HTTPD through read and close. Close detaches
 * its extension before final node disposal, including reentrant cleanup.
 * The request path is a local array; separate HTTP files can overlap subject
 * to their backend's contract.
 *
 * ROMFS descriptions and callback tables are immutable and outlive all
 * sessions. Dynamic and compression callbacks own their shared-state
 * synchronization; stream nodes similarly borrow backend interfaces whose
 * independent opens may alias the same cursor. Callback data buffers are
 * borrowed only for the call. These callbacks may inherit a scratch pair and
 * must not wait for another one, including by reentering an allocating root
 * operation. Native filesystem storage callbacks must not reenter VFS.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_buffers Shared buffer pairs
 *
 * Each shared pool element reserves two full path buffers atomically.
 * @p paths.path1 and @p paths.path2 each hold @p VFS_CFG_PATHLEN_MAX characters
 * plus the terminator; @p buf is a single scratch view of the same storage.
 * @p VFS_BUFFER_SIZE is the combined scratch capacity. The element also has
 * pointer alignment and may contain trailing alignment padding, which is not
 * part of that capacity. @p VFS_CFG_PATHBUFS_NUM counts pairs.
 *
 * @p vfs_buffer_take_wait() waits on the guarded pool without holding an
 * upper-layer VFS lock. It returns a whole pair, released with
 * @p vfs_buffer_release(). A caller retaining a pair must not wait for a
 * second pair from the same pool, including through delegated calls or
 * callbacks. Do not hold a lock needed by a thread returning a pair.
 *
 * Each root path operation allocates, uses, and returns its pair locally.
 * It reads CWD and routing state after allocation. Overlay and ROMFS consume
 * borrowed read-only paths without reserving another pair. A driver may
 * retain these paths throughout its call, including waits. Borrowed paths
 * expire on return.
 * Root is an outer entry point: do not stack a root below another root or
 * reenter an allocating root operation while retaining a pair.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_lifetime Object lifetime
 *
 * File-system objects derive from @p base_object_c and are not reference
 * counted. Their lifetime is managed by the caller. Overlays and roots borrow
 * their backing and registered file systems. Unregistering a file system only
 * removes its mapping, and disposing an overlay or root does not dispose any
 * backing or registered file systems. The same file system can therefore be
 * shared by multiple overlays or roots.
 *
 * Nodes are reference counted, but their owner pointers do not retain a
 * reference to the file system. Each file system, including an overlay or
 * root, must remain alive until all its nodes have been released and all its
 * operations have finished. Before disposing a file system, the caller must
 * also remove every mapping or backing relationship that could access it.
 * Unregistering a mapping does not close nodes previously opened through it.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_fs_paths File-system path contract
 *
 * Except for root entry points described below, every path passed to a
 * @p vfs_fs_c virtual method shall be:
 *
 * - absolute, beginning with a slash;
 * - normalized, with no repeated separators;
 * - free of @c "." and @c ".." elements;
 * - free of a trailing separator, except for the root path @c "/".
 *
 * Non-root file-system methods do not consult or modify a current directory.
 * Mounted file systems and nodes refer to @p vfs_fs_c objects, so the same
 * file system can be shared by multiple roots.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_root_paths Root path contract
 *
 * Root methods and the top-level path API accept both absolute and relative
 * paths. A root resolves relative inputs against its own current directory
 * and normalizes all input paths before calling the selected file system.
 * The top-level API uses the default root. A rename resolves both paths from
 * the same current-directory snapshot. Changing the current directory
 * preserves its logical candidate through validation and node cleanup before
 * committing it.
 * @p vfsRootOpen() keeps one resolved logical path across file-to-directory
 * fallback even if CWD changes while a driver sleeps. Use it for combined
 * opens through a root; @p vfsFSOpen() borrows a normalized absolute path.
 *
 * Root owns the optional backing prefix. Registered mounts bypass it; only
 * backing paths receive the prefix. Opening logical @c "/" merges registered
 * names with the backing directory at the prefix. CWD stays in the logical
 * namespace and never includes that prefix. @p ovldrvObjectInit() now takes
 * only the overlay and backing FS; @p vfsrootObjectInit() retains its prefix
 * argument. The prefix must remain valid for the lifetime of the root.
 *
 * Root path state is private to the root instance. Two roots may therefore
 * share mounted file systems without sharing their current directories.
 * Directory nodes remain per-open handles and are not file-system objects.
 */
